.he "find (1)                     8/16/26                     find (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
find
- find files
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
find path-list predicate-list
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
Find descends the directory tree under each name in the path-list,
looking for files that satisfy the predicate-list, which is a boolean
expression built from the primaries below.  A primary is tested
against the file being looked at, and prints or acts only when the
whole expression is true.
.sp
A number argument n is a decimal integer.  +n means more than n, -n
means less than n, and n means exactly n.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
PRIMARIES
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
-name name	True if the file name matches, using the shell's * ? and [ ] characters.

.ti -10
-perm onum	True if the permission bits are exactly onum; with -onum, the bits in onum must all be set.

.ti -10
-type c	True if the file is of type c, one of b, c, d or f for block device, character device, directory or plain file.

.ti -10
-links n	True if the file has n links.

.ti -10
-user name	True if the file belongs to name, a login name or numeric user id.

.ti -10
-group name	True if the file belongs to name, a group name or numeric group id.

.ti -10
-size n	True if the file is n blocks long, a block being 512 bytes.

.ti -10
-inum n	True if the file has inode number n.

.ti -10
-atime n	True if the file was read n days ago.

.ti -10
-mtime n	True if the file was written n days ago.

.ti -10
-newer file	True if the file was written more recently than file.

.ti -10
-exec command ;	Run command, replacing the argument { } with the pathname; true if the command exits zero.  The command must be a path - there is no PATH search.

.ti -10
-ok command ;	Like -exec, but write the command to the terminal and run it only after a y answer.

.ti -10
-print	Always true; print the pathname.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
OPERATORS
.in +5
.br
.in +10
.ti -10
! primary	True if primary is false.

.ti -10
primary -a primary	Both must be true; the -a may be left out, and two primaries written together are anded.

.ti -10
primary -o primary	Either may be true.

.ti -10
( expression )	Grouping; the parentheses must be quoted for the shell.

.in -10
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The port dropped two primaries the v7 find had.  -ctime is gone
because the micronix inode keeps no change time, and -cpio because it
needed fields the inode does not have and an endianness test that only
made sense between the PDP-11 and the VAX.
.sp
The path-list is looked at with stat, so a file argument works as well
as a directory: it is tested once and reported if it matches.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
ls (1), chmod (1), sh (1)
